sri lanka is running out of food money  and essentials  several people have been killed hundreds  have been injured the prime minister has  resigned and a nationwide curfew imposed  as the country erupted into violence  this island nation of 22 million people  is in a state of emergency these are the  reasons behind the crisis  there is no milk powder no  gas for cooking all the like small  businesses are getting close  the economy in sri lanka was already in  a precarious state before the most  recent unrest  it has significant debt and relies  heavily on gas imports  tourism is vital to help offset these  problems in sri lanka's economy and in  recent years two things torpedoed the  industry  in april 2019  the commercial capital of sri lanka  colombo was targeted in a series of  suicide bombings  islamic state took responsibility for  the attack  more than 250 people died and the  tourism industry was badly damaged  the covert 19 pandemic dealt a further  blow and it also affected other sectors  sri lanka suffered a significant loss of  income and jobs in major economic  sectors like garments and construction  government policies also crippled the  economy  in the wake of the easter bombings in  2019  an election was won by a president who  had made some big promises  his name was gutibaya rajapaksa  and his brother mahinda rajapaksa became  the prime minister  in december 2019 the government  announced it was cutting vat from 15  to just 8  it also abolished 7 other taxes they  were the largest tax cuts in sri lanka's  history but the former finance minister  now admits they led to the country  losing one million taxpayers  not only that the tax cuts led to a  credit rating downgrade the following  year  that meant sri lanka was not only losing  money it was also blocked from borrowing  more  that wasn't the only policy change in  2021 things were beginning to look up  the economy had picked up by eight  percent underpinned in part by a strong  growth in agriculture  but then in may 2021 president rajapaksa  announced a ban on the import of  chemical fertilizers  in a push to promote organic farming  the ban resulted in crop failure and  pushed up food prices  fast forward to today with the war in  ukraine making imports more expensive  the central bank says that the price of  food has increased by almost 50 percent  nationwide protests have been going on  for months  fuel shortages have led to country-wide  power cuts  and lower fruit and vegetable yields  a state of emergency has been declared  and troops deployed  and now almost all of sri lanka's  cabinet have resigned as has the prime  minister  sri lanka's recovery depends on getting  emergency loans and negotiating a  bailout from the international monetary  fund and that can only happen with  political stability
 

